            "output": "{\"body\":\"## Problem Statement\\n\\nThe nonprofit needs a consistent way to receive requests for Google Workspace accounts, review applicant information, choose initial Google Group memberships, and give approved applicants first-login access. Acceptance must not be confused with successful account creation or completed account handover. Administrators need to recover from partial failures without creating duplicate accounts, losing requests, or accidentally deleting successfully provisioned accounts.\\n\\nApplicants need a straightforward public form and email verification without needing a personal Google account or an applicant dashboard. The public submission and verification-email resend flows also need CAPTCHA protection.\\n\\nThe project currently has a starter Next.js interface and documented domain decisions, but no implemented onboarding workflow, authentication, application schema, or existing automated test suite. The user requires frontend-first development with the non-Google backend functionality ready in the initial stage. Only Google Workspace-dependent integrations are deferred.\\n\\n## Solution\\n\\nDeliver an English-language onboarding website for one nonprofit Google Workspace organization in two ordered phases:\\n\\n1. **Frontend and application functionality first:** Build the applicant and administrator experience first, then complete its non-Google backend in the same initial stage. Deliver real Neon Postgres storage through Drizzle, application HTTP operations, server-side validation, Cloudflare Turnstile verification, email-verification token handling, review and lifecycle transitions, session/security handling, retry/progress persistence, notification preparation, and expiry/cleanup. The frontend uses these real application operations. Only Google Workspace-dependent external responses remain simulated in development and acceptance scenarios. Cover validation, loading, empty, success, permission-denied, and failure/recovery states.\\n2. **Google Workspace integration later:** Connect the already-working application to the Google-dependent identity/Directory checks, administrator OAuth grants and actual resource permissions, account creation, group reads and memberships, and dedicated Gmail sender. Preserve the existing frontend, database, CAPTCHA, and workflow behavior. Google-dependent operations are not production-ready until this stage is verified with the live tenant.\\n\\nAn applicant submits a signup request and verifies their contact email. An authorized administrator corrects the request as needed, chooses an available Workspace email and zero or more initial group memberships, and accepts or rejects it. Acceptance starts provisioning. Once account creation and all selected memberships succeed, the administrator performs account handover through Google's Admin console and explicitly confirms that first-login instructions were sent.\\n\\n## User Stories\\n\\n1. As an applicant, I want to open a public signup form without signing in, so that I can request a Workspace account before I have one.\\n2. As an applicant, I want to submit my given name, family name, and contact email, so that an administrator has the required information to review my signup request.\\n3. As an applicant, I want to optionally supply a phone number and explain my connection to the nonprofit, so that I can provide useful context without unnecessary required fields.\\n4. As an applicant, I want clear field-level validation, so that I can correct missing or invalid information before submission.\\n5. As an applicant, I want an accessible English-language form that works on mobile and desktop, so that I can complete signup with my usual device and input method.\\n6. As an applicant, I want CAPTCHA with minimal friction, so that legitimate requests remain easy while automated submissions are blocked.\\n7. As an applicant, I want to retain entered form values when CAPTCHA or submission fails, so that I can retry without filling out the form again.\\n8. As an applicant, I want clear submission progress and confirmation, so that I know whether my signup request was received.\\n9. As an applicant, I want a verification email at my contact email, so that I can demonstrate ownership without a personal Google account.\\n10. As an applicant, I want to follow a verification link without another CAPTCHA challenge, so that confirming my email is straightforward.\\n11. As an applicant, I want clear outcomes for valid, expired, invalid, and already-used verification links, so that I understand whether verification succeeded or what to do next.\\n12. As an applicant, I want to request a replacement verification email after completing CAPTCHA, so that I can recover from a missing message without enabling automated resend abuse.\\n13. As an applicant, I want to know that an unverified submission expires after 24 hours and may require a fresh submission, so that I understand the verification deadline.\\n14. As an applicant, I want duplicate submissions not to overwrite my active signup request, so that my original information and review progress remain intact.\\n15. As an applicant, I want to submit a fresh request after rejection or expiry, so that I can reapply when appropriate.\\n16. As an applicant, I want a rejection email containing an explanation, so that I understand the administrator's decision.\\n17. As an approved applicant, I want first-login instructions after my account and selected memberships are ready, so that I receive usable access.\\n18. As an approved applicant, I want to change my password at first login, so that I establish my own credentials.\\n19. As an applicant, I want my form data removed at the agreed lifecycle boundaries, so that the website does not indefinitely retain my submitted personal information.\\n20. As an administrator, I want to sign in with my managed Google identity, so that my existing Workspace authority governs management actions.\\n21. As an administrator, I want explicit unavailable, access-denied, and reconnection states, so that credential or Directory visibility failures do not appear as an empty queue or successful access.\\n22. As an administrator, I want a queue containing only verified requests eligible for review, so that I do not act on unverified submissions.\\n23. As an administrator, I want clear loading, empty, and queue-load failure states, so that I can distinguish no work from unavailable data.\\n24. As an administrator, I want to inspect the submitted applicant information, so that I can make an informed acceptance or rejection decision.\\n25. As an administrator, I want to correct submitted information before acceptance, so that provisioning uses reviewed information.\\n26. As an administrator, I want to confirm a corrected contact email without requiring the applicant to verify the replacement, so that I can apply the agreed administrator-confirmed contact email policy.\\n27. As an administrator, I want a suggested Workspace email based on given name and family name, so that account naming requires less repetitive input.\\n28. As an administrator, I want to confirm or edit the Workspace email before acceptance, so that the chosen address is intentional.\\n29. As an administrator, I want an address collision to require another Workspace email, so that an applicant is never attached to an unrelated existing account.\\n30. As an administrator, I want to select only existing Google Groups I can manage, so that I make assignments within my actual authority.\\n31. As an administrator, I want to accept a request with zero groups, so that group membership is optional rather than automatically assigned.\\n32. As an administrator, I want to select multiple initial groups, so that the new Workspace user receives all reviewed memberships.\\n33. As an administrator, I want each selected group's role to default to member, so that ordinary membership is the default.\\n34. As an administrator, I want to explicitly select manager or owner when appropriate, so that elevated group roles are intentional.\\n35. As an administrator, I want group roles and notification-group membership to remain distinct from administrative authority, so that neither can improperly grant application administration.\\n36. As an administrator, I want to review the final account address and memberships before accepting, so that I can verify the proposed provisioning outcome.\\n37. As an administrator, I want to reject a request with a required applicant-facing reason, so that the applicant receives a meaningful outcome.\\n38. As an administrator, I want repeated or concurrent review actions to avoid conflicting decisions and duplicate accounts, so that the workflow remains consistent when multiple administrators work.\\n39. As an administrator, I want acceptance, provisioning, and account handover to appear as distinct milestones, so that an accepted request is not mistaken for completed onboarding.\\n40. As an administrator, I want provisioning progress and outstanding errors to be visible, so that I know which steps succeeded and which still need attention.\\n41. As an administrator, I want partial failures to retain the account and successful memberships, so that recovery does not destroy completed work.\\n42. As an administrator, I want to retry only unfinished provisioning steps, so that retries cannot create another account or repeat successful assignments unnecessarily.\\n43. As an administrator, I want to remove or replace an unfinished assignment when a selected group disappears or becomes unmanageable, so that onboarding can proceed with an explicitly revised selection.\\n44. As an administrator, I want revisions recorded and confirmed while successful memberships remain intact, so that recovery is deliberate and traceable.\\n45. As an administrator, I want handover blocked until all currently selected memberships succeed, so that first-login instructions are not sent for incomplete provisioning.\\n46. As an administrator, I want an Awaiting handover state after provisioning succeeds, so that I know the next required action.\\n47. As an administrator, I want guidance to use Google's Admin console password-reset and email flow, so that account handover follows the existing secure process.\\n48. As an administrator, I want to explicitly confirm that first-login instructions were sent, so that onboarding completion does not depend on an unsupported automatic delivery assumption.\\n49. As an administrator, I want confirmation to mean instructions were sent rather than the applicant has signed in, so that the recorded completion has a precise meaning.\\n50. As an administrator, I want notification of newly verified signup requests with a review-queue link and minimal personal information, so that I can find work without exposing unnecessary applicant details in email.\\n51. As an administrator, I want failed notification delivery to leave requests in the queue and expose a retry action, so that email failure does not lose review work.\\n52. As an administrator, I want failed rejection notification to retain the necessary request data until successful delivery, so that the applicant can still receive the decision.\\n53. As an administrator, I want unreviewed verified requests to expire after 30 days, so that old requests do not remain pending indefinitely.\\n54. As an administrator, I want accepted requests awaiting recovery or handover excluded from unreviewed-request expiry, so that cleanup cannot erase recovery state for an already-created account.\\n55. As the nonprofit, I want successful applicant form data removed after confirmed handover while retaining a minimal operational record, so that onboarding records do not become a parallel Workspace profile database.\\n56. As a reviewer, I want to inspect the complete frontend with real application persistence and CAPTCHA while Google Workspace responses are simulated, so that the application can be validated before Google integration is available.\\n57. As a developer, I want stable behavior-oriented HTTP contracts backed by the non-Google application functionality in the first stage, so that later Google Workspace integration does not require redesigning screens or replacing fake database and CAPTCHA behavior.\\n58. As a developer, I want the same browser acceptance scenarios exercised before and after Google Workspace integration, so that deferred provider integration does not leave application or Google behavior unverified.\\n\\n## Implementation Decisions\\n\\n- **Delivery order is mandatory:** Build the frontend first, then finish the non-Google application backend before the later Google Workspace integration stage. Temporary frontend HTTP fixtures may help develop screens, but they are not the first-stage deliverable: the initial stage must run against real application endpoints, database persistence, server-side validation, and Turnstile verification. Only Google Workspace-dependent external calls remain simulated. Full production completion additionally requires the verified Google integrations.\\n- **Existing foundations:** Use the existing Next.js application, shadcn components, and Tailwind. Use Vercel and Neon Postgres with Drizzle. Read the installed Next.js guides before implementation and respect ADR-0001, ADR-0002, and ADR-0003.\\n- **Frontend surfaces:** Build the public signup and verification/resend experience, Google sign-in/access states, verified-request review queue and detail/review experience, provisioning progress and recovery, notification retry feedback, and Awaiting handover/completion experience. Prefer existing components and simple composition rather than speculative abstractions.\\n- **Deferred integration boundary:** Keep development simulations at the Google Workspace-dependent external boundary, not at the application database, CAPTCHA, or workflow boundary. Simulate Google identity/Directory results, permissions, account/group operations, and Gmail transport as necessary; exercise real application session, authorization-decision, persistence, review, retry, and cleanup logic around those results. Provide representative states including zero/multiple groups, roles, address collisions, denied access, partial provisioning, unavailable/deleted groups, notification failure, and handover. Use a local mail capture for Google-deferred email delivery so verification links and message contents can be exercised. Do not introduce application-owned administrator roles, a permanent alternate email provider, or mock-specific component business logic. Simulations must be explicitly development/test-only and must not imply that real Google operations or email delivery occurred.\\n- **Frontend/backend contract:** Define operations for signup submission, verification-email resend, email verification, authenticated queue/detail reads, permitted request corrections, group choices, acceptance, rejection, unfinished-step retries, unfinished-assignment revisions, notification retries, and handover confirmation. Keep authoritative state, validation outcomes, actionable failures, and progress explicit. Use only the fields each caller may see; never expose provider credentials, temporary account passwords, or administrator-only data through public responses.\\n- **Workflow ownership:** The initial stage implements the application-side authentication/session and authorization handling, signup review and lifecycle persistence, provisioning/recovery orchestration, email creation and retry state, CAPTCHA validation, and expiry/cleanup. The later stage supplies the real Google-dependent grants, identity/Directory checks, provisioning operations, and Gmail delivery. Keep orchestration cohesive behind the public application boundary; do not build a framework of interchangeable internal providers.\\n- **Signup data:** Require given name, family name, and contact email. Phone and connection explanation are optional. Deliver server-side input validation and frontend validation in the initial stage; neither is deferred to Google integration.\\n- **Verification and expiry:** Verify contact-email ownership before review eligibility. Verification links expire after 24 hours. An unverified submission is deleted 24 hours after initial submission; resends do not extend this lifetime. A replacement link is usable only while its underlying submission remains eligible. Expire unreviewed verified requests after 30 days of entering the verified review queue. Accepted requests awaiting provisioning recovery or handover are not subject to that unreviewed expiry.\\n- **Duplicate requests:** Enforce at most one active signup request per verified contact email through database-backed constraints and concurrency-safe transitions. A duplicate submission cannot overwrite the active request, and verification must not silently create competing active requests. Permit fresh requests after rejection or expiry. Contact-email matches alone do not establish existing Workspace identity.\\n- **CAPTCHA:** Use Cloudflare Turnstile managed mode on signup submission and verification-email resend. Verify tokens server-side through Siteverify before accepting the protected operation, checking the intended hostname and action and keeping the secret server-side. Missing, invalid, expired, replayed, or unverifiable tokens cannot perform the protected action. Tokens expire after five minutes and are single-use. Verification-link clicks and signed-in administrator actions are CAPTCHA-free. The frontend must retain entered values and provide accessible retry feedback.\\n- **Authoritative identity and authority:** Google is authoritative for managed users, profiles, groups, memberships, and administrative privileges. Administrative operations use the acting administrator OAuth grant and its actual permissions/resource restrictions. Never retry denied operations with stronger credentials. Group manager/owner roles and notification-group membership do not confer application administration.\\n- **Protected access:** Follow ADR-0002's successful current own-profile lookup requirement using the authenticated identity and the permitted Directory view. Hidden, unavailable, or deleted profiles and rejected credentials produce explicit unavailable/reconnection outcomes. Do not substitute a shared reader or private/admin-view fallback. This check is not an instantaneous suspended-status detector.\\n- **OAuth and email grants:** Follow ADR-0002's user/group management scopes. Verification, outcome, and administrator notification emails use the dedicated Workspace mailbox's separate offline OAuth grant limited to gmail.send. All credentials remain server-side.\\n- **Administrator corrections:** Administrators may correct applicant form information before acceptance. A corrected contact email may be administrator-confirmed without applicant reverification, with that distinction retained during the workflow. Corrections must respect active-request uniqueness.\\n- **Workspace email selection:** Suggest given.family at the configured Workspace domain; require administrator confirmation or editing. Invalid names or address collisions require an explicit valid available address. Never use a matching pre-existing account as the provisioning result without evidence that it was created by this accepted request.\\n- **Initial group memberships:** Offer existing groups the administrator can manage. Zero groups is valid and no assignments are automatic. Default to member; manager/owner require explicit selection. Persist selected group identities and roles for resumable provisioning rather than relying on display names.\\n- **Lifecycle:** Keep unverified submission, verified awaiting-review, acceptance/provisioning, Awaiting handover, confirmed completion, rejection awaiting notification, and expiry distinguishable. Acceptance records the reviewed choices and authorizes provisioning; it is not completion. A rejection includes a required applicant-facing reason.\\n- **Persistence:** Store submitted form data, verification evidence/deadlines, lifecycle state, reviewed account address, selected memberships/roles, approving administrator identity, resulting Google account identity, unfinished-operation progress, notification outcomes, and outstanding-assignment revisions only as needed for the workflow. Use Drizzle migrations and database-backed uniqueness/concurrency controls. Do not store parallel authoritative Workspace profiles or application-owned administrator roles.\\n- **Concurrent actions and uncertain results:** Repeated submissions, double-clicks, simultaneous review, retries, process interruption, and external API timeouts must not create conflicting decisions or duplicate accounts. Persist and reconcile durable provisioning progress with Google before treating an uncertain create outcome as safe to repeat. An address match by itself is insufficient to claim ownership of an account.\\n- **Partial provisioning recovery:** Create the account and establish selected initial memberships. Keep successful work on failure, show the unfinished operations, and let an authorized administrator retry only unfinished steps. Never automatically delete an account to roll back failed onboarding. Recovery uses the current acting administrator's permitted credentials, not a stored stronger identity.\\n- **Assignment revisions:** An authorized administrator may explicitly remove or replace an unfinished assignment when a group disappears or becomes unmanageable. Preserve already-successful memberships, record the revision while the workflow is active, and require confirmation. Do not silently drop failed groups. All currently selected memberships must succeed before handover; zero remaining selections is valid when explicitly confirmed.\\n- **Account handover:** Show Awaiting handover only after provisioning succeeds. The administrator performs Google's Admin console existing-user password-reset/email flow with a required password change, then confirms in the website that instructions were sent. Do not email plaintext passwords through the application. Confirmation is not proof of inbox delivery or first sign-in.\\n- **Notifications:** Notify the configured Google Group when a verified request enters the review queue using minimal personal information and a review link. Its membership controls delivery only. Notification failure does not prevent queue entry, erase the request, or undo account creation; show failure and permit retry. Retain rejection form data until its outcome notification succeeds.\\n- **Cleanup and retained records:** Delete unverified submissions at their deadline, expire unreviewed verified requests at their deadline, delete rejected applicant profiles after successful outcome notification, and delete successful applicant form data after provisioning and confirmed handover. Retain only the minimal operational record identifying the resulting Google account and approving administrator after success. Active recovery/revision records must not become a permanent second profile system. Cleanup must be resumable and must not delete active accepted-request recovery state.\\n- **Deployment configuration:** Configure the Workspace organization/domain, Google OAuth grants/redirects, sender mailbox, notification group, Neon connection, and Turnstile site key, secret, and permitted hostnames. Keep secrets out of Git. Missing required production configuration cannot silently enable mock operations or bypass validation.\\n- **Repository delivery:** Implement through the repository's worktree and stacked-PR workflow: frontend development first, complete application/database/CAPTCHA functionality next, and Google Workspace integration last. Use small functional commits, review for correctness and unnecessary complexity, run meaningful checks/lint/formatting, and link resulting PRs back to this issue.\\n\\n## Testing Decisions\\n\\n- **User-confirmed seam:** Test the running website at its public interface. Browser flows are the primary seam; use the same application HTTP boundary for concurrency, repeated actions, and cleanup checks. Avoid separate suites for every internal helper or component.\\n- **What makes a good test:** Assert externally observable behavior and meaningful consequences: available actions, validation messages, lifecycle visibility, delivered email contents, account/membership operations, retained successful work, retry outcomes, and cleanup boundaries. Do not assert component hierarchy, private helper calls, internal state-machine representation, or incidental request ordering.\\n- **Existing prior art:** The starter application has no existing automated tests, test runner configuration, onboarding implementation, or established test seam to reuse. Introduce the smallest browser/HTTP acceptance setup needed for these scenarios, rather than a broad testing framework or redundant per-layer suites.\\n- **Frontend-first development checks:** Temporary deterministic HTTP fixtures may be used while composing screens. Cover signup validation and CAPTCHA feedback; verification/resend outcomes; loading/empty/error queue states; corrected information; Workspace address collision; zero/multiple groups and explicit roles; review confirmation; rejection; provisioning failure/retry; unfinished-assignment revision; notification failure/retry; and manual handover confirmation. Include keyboard operation, labels, focus/error feedback, and responsive layout checks. Fixture-only checks do not establish first-stage completion.\\n- **Initial-stage application checks:** Run those browser scenarios against real application endpoints and an isolated real Postgres database before Google Workspace integration. Simulate Google-dependent OAuth/Directory/Gmail responses at their external boundary; use captured messages to exercise real email-verification links. Do not replace application session/authorization handling, workflow orchestration, persistence, or server-side CAPTCHA validation with mocks. Exercise Turnstile validation through its external boundary using its documented testing configuration or deterministic service responses, and verify real configured Turnstile integration before claiming the initial-stage CAPTCHA deliverable is ready. Recorded Google simulations establish expected application behavior, not real Workspace provisioning or email delivery.\\n- **Later Google integration checks:** Reuse the same browser/HTTP scenarios after replacing the Google simulations with real integrations. The already-implemented database, CAPTCHA, concurrency controls, verification, review, notification retry, and retention behavior must continue to pass. Keep deterministic provider-failure scenarios at the external service boundary; validate actual Google permissions and transport with the live-tenant smoke check.\\n- **Protected actions:** Verify that missing/invalid/expired/replayed CAPTCHA or Siteverify failure cannot create a submission or send a resend email, while verification-link clicks remain CAPTCHA-free. Verify rejected credentials, unavailable own-profile lookup, unauthorized management, and Google-denied operations cannot proceed or fall back to stronger credentials.\\n- **Verification and retention:** Check verification deadlines, non-extending resends, duplicate verification races, reapplication, verified review eligibility, the 30-day review deadline, rejection-notification-before-deletion, completion-before-deletion, and retention of accepted partial-provisioning/handover state. Arrange aged persisted records rather than waiting for wall-clock deadlines.\\n- **Concurrency and recovery:** Issue parallel or repeated operations through the application HTTP boundary. Demonstrate at most one active verified request, a single accepted outcome/account, address-collision rejection without existing-account attachment, recovery after an uncertain create response, partial group failure retaining successful assignments, explicit revision of unfinished groups, and no automatic account deletion.\\n- **Handover and notifications:** Prove handover remains blocked until selected memberships succeed, completion requires explicit confirmation, and email failures neither lose requests nor undo provisioned accounts. Confirm notification-group membership and group manager/owner roles never grant administrative authority.\\n- **Live Workspace smoke check:** With configured tenant credentials, verify real OAuth permissions, administrator resource restrictions, group roles, sender/notification-group delivery, the Admin console handover flow, and suspended/deleted-account behavior with existing sessions. This check is separate from deterministic automated acceptance tests because mocked services cannot establish actual tenant policy or Google propagation timing.\\n- **Phase gates:** Initial-stage completion requires the frontend running against real application endpoints with working database persistence, CAPTCHA, verification workflow, review transitions, concurrency/recovery state, notification handling, and retention/cleanup, plus appropriate browser/HTTP security/concurrency/retention checks and build/lint/format checks. Only Google-dependent external responses may remain simulated. Later integration completion requires the reused checks and a documented live-tenant Google smoke-check outcome. Do not label a UI-only mock, fake database, or widget-only CAPTCHA implementation as first-stage completion, or simulated Google operations as production integration.\\n\\n## Out of Scope\\n\\n- Applicant accounts, applicant sign-in, or an applicant dashboard.\\n- Ongoing Workspace user management, suspension, deletion, existing-user group management, or group creation/deletion.\\n- Self-service contact changes and profile change requests, although existing ADRs still govern future work.\\n- Multiple Workspace organizations, localization, or a language switcher.\\n- Automatic initial group assignments or onboarding into pre-existing accounts based solely on email matches.\\n- Application-owned administrator roles, parallel authoritative Workspace profiles, domain-wide delegation, or stronger-credential fallback.\\n- Automatic plaintext-password emails, automatic handover confirmation, proof of inbox delivery, or first-login tracking.\\n- Reversal of successful provisioning through automatic account deletion.\\n- Hosting tier management, automatic provider upgrades, independent backups beyond the existing hosting decision, or speculative generalized infrastructure.\\n\\n## Further Notes\\n\\n- This specification synthesizes the completed design conversation. The user confirmed the website-boundary testing seam and clarified that frontend-first development must include the non-Google backend functionality before the later Google Workspace integration; further product interviewing is not required.\\n- Respect the domain glossary and the existing decisions: Google-authoritative identity/profiles, acting-administrator OAuth with the dedicated sender grant, and Vercel/Neon hosting. The release includes onboarding only; broader documented capabilities remain future scope.\\n- The existing issue is being updated rather than creating a competing specification. It is ready for implementation once labeled ready-for-agent.\\n- The initial stage needs real database and Turnstile configuration and must be independently runnable without Google Workspace credentials, with clearly development-only Google simulations. Full deployment additionally needs organization-specific Google configuration and OAuth grants. The dedicated Gmail transport is part of the deferred Google integration; email preparation, verification tokens, captured-message testing, notification state, and retry behavior are implemented in the initial stage rather than switching the agreed production email provider.\\n- Cloudflare's server-side validation documentation establishes the Siteverify requirement, five-minute token lifetime, and single-use behavior: https://developers.cloudflare.com/turnstile/get-started/server-side-validation/.\\n- A manual account-handover step is deliberate. Google remains authoritative, and the website records an administrator's confirmation that instructions were sent.\\n\",\"number\":1,\"title\":\"Implement Google Workspace signup review and onboarding\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/itsmeyaw/binden/issues/1\"}\n{\"body\":\"## Parent\\n\\n#1\\n\\n## What to build\\n\\nDeliver a public applicant path that submits one signup request through the running website. It persists the request in Neon Postgres through Drizzle, validates fields on the server, validates Cloudflare Turnstile server-side, prevents a competing active request, and records a verification message in local mail capture.\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n\\n- [ ] An applicant can submit required and optional data from mobile or desktop with accessible field-level validation and progress feedback.\\n- [ ] Invalid, expired, replayed, or unverifiable Turnstile tokens cannot create a signup request, and recoverable failures retain entered values.\\n- [ ] The persisted application prevents a duplicate submission from overwriting an active signup request and exposes a clear outcome.\\n- [ ] A captured verification message is created without requiring Google Workspace credentials.\\n\\n## Blocked by\\n\\nNone - can start immediately\",\"comments\":[{\"id\":\"IC_kwDOU7HV788AAAABZGOn9A\",\"author\":{\"login\":\"itsmeyaw\"},\"authorAssociation\":\"OWNER\",\"body\":\"Implemented the protected public signup path in stack #16. Persistence and migration: #13. Zod validation, server-side Turnstile verification, atomic mail-capture write, and HTTP tests: #14. Landing sign-in/signup choice and the sign-up form: #15. Checks passed: formatting, TypeScript, focused signup tests, Oxlint, production build, and browser route/mobile smoke checks. Real Neon writes require a configured DATABASE_URL.\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-10-04T10:55:04Z\",\"includesCreatedEdit\":true,\"isMinimized\":false,\"minimizedReason\":\"\",\"reactionGroups\":[],\"url\":\"https://github.com/itsmeyaw/binden/issues/2#issuecomment-5979219956\",\"viewerDidAuthor\":true},{\"id\":\"IC_kwDOU7HV788AAAABZGT9cg\",\"author\":{\"login\":\"itsmeyaw\"},\"authorAssociation\":\"OWNER\",\"body\":\"Follow-up: PR #15 now explicitly approves the required esbuild install scripts in the pnpm 11 workspace policy, so pnpm install and pnpm dev run normally.\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-10-04T11:07:27Z\",\"includesCreatedEdit\":false,\"isMinimized\":false,\"minimizedReason\":\"\",\"reactionGroups\":[],\"url\":\"https://github.com/itsmeyaw/binden/issues/2#issuecomment-5979307378\",\"viewerDidAuthor\":true},{\"id\":\"IC_kwDOU7HV788AAAABZGWswQ\",\"author\":{\"login\":\"itsmeyaw\"},\"authorAssociation\":\"OWNER\",\"body\":\"Follow-up: PR #15 now loads .env.local and .env for drizzle-kit, so the local DATABASE_URL is available when running pnpm exec drizzle-kit migrate.\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-10-04T11:13:55Z\",\"includesCreatedEdit\":false,\"isMinimized\":false,\"minimizedReason\":\"\",\"reactionGroups\":[],\"url\":\"https://github.com/itsmeyaw/binden/issues/2#issuecomment-5979352257\",\"viewerDidAuthor\":true},{\"id\":\"IC_kwDOU7HV788AAAABZG--fQ\",\"author\":{\"login\":\"itsmeyaw\"},\"authorAssociation\":\"OWNER\",\"body\":\"Follow-up: PR #15 now forces the Turnstile widget to the light theme and adds extra bottom padding below the submit action.\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-10-04T12:39:31Z\",\"includesCreatedEdit\":false,\"isMinimized\":false,\"minimizedReason\":\"\",\"reactionGroups\":[],\"url\":\"https://github.com/itsmeyaw/binden/issues/2#issuecomment-5980012157\",\"viewerDidAuthor\":true},{\"id\":\"IC_kwDOU7HV788AAAABZHB9Ew\",\"author\":{\"login\":\"itsmeyaw\"},\"authorAssociation\":\"OWNER\",\"body\":\"Follow-up: PR #15 now follows shadcn `signup-01` layout: a full-viewport centered `max-w-sm` form card with a full-width in-form submit action.\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-10-04T12:45:01Z\",\"includesCreatedEdit\":false,\"isMinimized\":false,\"minimizedReason\":\"\",\"reactionGroups\":[],\"url\":\"https://github.com/itsmeyaw/binden/issues/2#issuecomment-5980060947\",\"viewerDidAuthor\":true},{\"id\":\"IC_kwDOU7HV788AAAABZHMXLA\",\"author\":{\"login\":\"itsmeyaw\"},\"authorAssociation\":\"OWNER\",\"body\":\"Follow-up: PR #15 now uses the shadcn `login-05` layout on the front page, branded for PM3 Muenchen e.V. with a `Login with Google Workspace` action and account-request link.\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-10-04T13:03:26Z\",\"includesCreatedEdit\":false,\"isMinimized\":false,\"minimizedReason\":\"\",\"reactionGroups\":[],\"url\":\"https://github.com/itsmeyaw/binden/issues/2#issuecomment-5980231468\",\"viewerDidAuthor\":true}],\"number\":2,\"title\":\"Submit a protected signup request\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/itsmeyaw/binden/issues/2\"}\n",
            "metadata": {
              "output": "...\n\nn decision.\\n25. As an administrator, I want to correct submitted information before acceptance, so that provisioning uses reviewed information.\\n26. As an administrator, I want to confirm a corrected contact email without requiring the applicant to verify the replacement, so that I can apply the agreed administrator-confirmed contact email policy.\\n27. As an administrator, I want a suggested Workspace email based on given name and family name, so that account naming requires less repetitive input.\\n28. As an administrator, I want to confirm or edit the Workspace email before acceptance, so that the chosen address is intentional.\\n29. As an administrator, I want an address collision to require another Workspace email, so that an applicant is never attached to an unrelated existing account.\\n30. As an administrator, I want to select only existing Google Groups I can manage, so that I make assignments within my actual authority.\\n31. As an administrator, I want to accept a request with zero groups, so that group membership is optional rather than automatically assigned.\\n32. As an administrator, I want to select multiple initial groups, so that the new Workspace user receives all reviewed memberships.\\n33. As an administrator, I want each selected group's role to default to member, so that ordinary membership is the default.\\n34. As an administrator, I want to explicitly select manager or owner when appropriate, so that elevated group roles are intentional.\\n35. As an administrator, I want group roles and notification-group membership to remain distinct from administrative authority, so that neither can improperly grant application administration.\\n36. As an administrator, I want to review the final account address and memberships before accepting, so that I can verify the proposed provisioning outcome.\\n37. As an administrator, I want to reject a request with a required applicant-facing reason, so that the applicant receives a meaningful outcome.\\n38. As an administrator, I want repeated or concurrent review actions to avoid conflicting decisions and duplicate accounts, so that the workflow remains consistent when multiple administrators work.\\n39. As an administrator, I want acceptance, provisioning, and account handover to appear as distinct milestones, so that an accepted request is not mistaken for completed onboarding.\\n40. As an administrator, I want provisioning progress and outstanding errors to be visible, so that I know which steps succeeded and which still need attention.\\n41. As an administrator, I want partial failures to retain the account and successful memberships, so that recovery does not destroy completed work.\\n42. As an administrator, I want to retry only unfinished provisioning steps, so that retries cannot create another account or repeat successful assignments unnecessarily.\\n43. As an administrator, I want to remove or replace an unfinished assignment when a selected group disappears or becomes unmanageable, so that onboarding can proceed with an explicitly revised selection.\\n44. As an administrator, I want revisions recorded and confirmed while successful memberships remain intact, so that recovery is deliberate and traceable.\\n45. As an administrator, I want handover blocked until all currently selected memberships succeed, so that first-login instructions are not sent for incomplete provisioning.\\n46. As an administrator, I want an Awaiting handover state after provisioning succeeds, so that I know the next required action.\\n47. As an administrator, I want guidance to use Google's Admin console password-reset and email flow, so that account handover follows the existing secure process.\\n48. As an administrator, I want to explicitly confirm that first-login instructions were sent, so that onboarding completion does not depend on an unsupported automatic delivery assumption.\\n49. As an administrator, I want confirmation to mean instructions were sent rather than the applicant has signed in, so that the recorded completion has a precise meaning.\\n50. As an administrator, I want notification of newly verified signup requests with a review-queue link and minimal personal information, so that I can find work without exposing unnecessary applicant details in email.\\n51. As an administrator, I want failed notification delivery to leave requests in the queue and expose a retry action, so that email failure does not lose review work.\\n52. As an administrator, I want failed rejection notification to retain the necessary request data until successful delivery, so that the applicant can still receive the decision.\\n53. As an administrator, I want unreviewed verified requests to expire after 30 days, so that old requests do not remain pending indefinitely.\\n54. As an administrator, I want accepted requests awaiting recovery or handover excluded from unreviewed-request expiry, so that cleanup cannot erase recovery state for an already-created account.\\n55. As the nonprofit, I want successful applicant form data removed after confirmed handover while retaining a minimal operational record, so that onboarding records do not become a parallel Workspace profile database.\\n56. As a reviewer, I want to inspect the complete frontend with real application persistence and CAPTCHA while Google Workspace responses are simulated, so that the application can be validated before Google integration is available.\\n57. As a developer, I want stable behavior-oriented HTTP contracts backed by the non-Google application functionality in the first stage, so that later Google Workspace integration does not require redesigning screens or replacing fake database and CAPTCHA behavior.\\n58. As a developer, I want the same browser acceptance scenarios exercised before and after Google Workspace integration, so that deferred provider integration does not leave application or Google behavior unverified.\\n\\n## Implementation Decisions\\n\\n- **Delivery order is mandatory:** Build the frontend first, then finish the non-Google application backend before the later Google Workspace integration stage. Temporary frontend HTTP fixtures may help develop screens, but they are not the first-stage deliverable: the initial stage must run against real application endpoints, database persistence, server-side validation, and Turnstile verification. Only Google Workspace-dependent external calls remain simulated. Full production completion additionally requires the verified Google integrations.\\n- **Existing foundations:** Use the existing Next.js application, shadcn components, and Tailwind. Use Vercel and Neon Postgres with Drizzle. Read the installed Next.js guides before implementation and respect ADR-0001, ADR-0002, and ADR-0003.\\n- **Frontend surfaces:** Build the public signup and verification/resend experience, Google sign-in/access states, verified-request review queue and detail/review experience, provisioning progress and recovery, notification retry feedback, and Awaiting handover/completion experience. Prefer existing components and simple composition rather than speculative abstractions.\\n- **Deferred integration boundary:** Keep development simulations at the Google Workspace-dependent external boundary, not at the application database, CAPTCHA, or workflow boundary. Simulate Google identity/Directory results, permissions, account/group operations, and Gmail transport as necessary; exercise real application session, authorization-decision, persistence, review, retry, and cleanup logic around those results. Provide representative states including zero/multiple groups, roles, address collisions, denied access, partial provisioning, unavailable/deleted groups, notification failure, and handover. Use a local mail capture for Google-deferred email delivery so verification links and message contents can be exercised. Do not introduce application-owned administrator roles, a permanent alternate email provider, or mock-specific component business logic. Simulations must be explicitly development/test-only and must not imply that real Google operations or email delivery occurred.\\n- **Frontend/backend contract:** Define operations for signup submission, verification-email resend, email verification, authenticated queue/detail reads, permitted request corrections, group choices, acceptance, rejection, unfinished-step retries, unfinished-assignment revisions, notification retries, and handover confirmation. Keep authoritative state, validation outcomes, actionable failures, and progress explicit. Use only the fields each caller may see; never expose provider credentials, temporary account passwords, or administrator-only data through public responses.\\n- **Workflow ownership:** The initial stage implements the application-side authentication/session and authorization handling, signup review and lifecycle persistence, provisioning/recovery orchestration, email creation and retry state, CAPTCHA validation, and expiry/cleanup. The later stage supplies the real Google-dependent grants, identity/Directory checks, provisioning operations, and Gmail delivery. Keep orchestration cohesive behind the public application boundary; do not build a framework of interchangeable internal providers.\\n- **Signup data:** Require given name, family name, and contact email. Phone and connection explanation are optional. Deliver server-side input validation and frontend validation in the initial stage; neither is deferred to Google integration.\\n- **Verification and expiry:** Verify contact-email ownership before review eligibility. Verification links expire after 24 hours. An unverified submission is deleted 24 hours after initial submission; resends do not extend this lifetime. A replacement link is usable only while its underlying submission remains eligible. Expire unreviewed verified requests after 30 days of entering the verified review queue. Accepted requests awaiting provisioning recovery or handover are not subject to that unreviewed expiry.\\n- **Duplicate requests:** Enforce at most one active signup request per verified contact email through database-backed constraints and concurrency-safe transitions. A duplicate submission cannot overwrite the active request, and verification must not silently create competing active requests. Permit fresh requests after rejection or expiry. Contact-email matches alone do not establish existing Workspace identity.\\n- **CAPTCHA:** Use Cloudflare Turnstile managed mode on signup submission and verification-email resend. Verify tokens server-side through Siteverify before accepting the protected operation, checking the intended hostname and action and keeping the secret server-side. Missing, invalid, expired, replayed, or unverifiable tokens cannot perform the protected action. Tokens expire after five minutes and are single-use. Verification-link clicks and signed-in administrator actions are CAPTCHA-free. The frontend must retain entered values and provide accessible retry feedback.\\n- **Authoritative identity and authority:** Google is authoritative for managed users, profiles, groups, memberships, and administrative privileges. Administrative operations use the acting administrator OAuth grant and its actual permissions/resource restrictions. Never retry denied operations with stronger credentials. Group manager/owner roles and notification-group membership do not confer application administration.\\n- **Protected access:** Follow ADR-0002's successful current own-profile lookup requirement using the authenticated identity and the permitted Directory view. Hidden, unavailable, or deleted profiles and rejected credentials produce explicit unavailable/reconnection outcomes. Do not substitute a shared reader or private/admin-view fallback. This check is not an instantaneous suspended-status detector.\\n- **OAuth and email grants:** Follow ADR-0002's user/group management scopes. Verification, outcome, and administrator notification emails use the dedicated Workspace mailbox's separate offline OAuth grant limited to gmail.send. All credentials remain server-side.\\n- **Administrator corrections:** Administrators may correct applicant form information before acceptance. A corrected contact email may be administrator-confirmed without applicant reverification, with that distinction retained during the workflow. Corrections must respect active-request uniqueness.\\n- **Workspace email selection:** Suggest given.family at the configured Workspace domain; require administrator confirmation or editing. Invalid names or address collisions require an explicit valid available address. Never use a matching pre-existing account as the provisioning result without evidence that it was created by this accepted request.\\n- **Initial group memberships:** Offer existing groups the administrator can manage. Zero groups is valid and no assignments are automatic. Default to member; manager/owner require explicit selection. Persist selected group identities and roles for resumable provisioning rather than relying on display names.\\n- **Lifecycle:** Keep unverified submission, verified awaiting-review, acceptance/provisioning, Awaiting handover, confirmed completion, rejection awaiting notification, and expiry distinguishable. Acceptance records the reviewed choices and authorizes provisioning; it is not completion. A rejection includes a required applicant-facing reason.\\n- **Persistence:** Store submitted form data, verification evidence/deadlines, lifecycle state, reviewed account address, selected memberships/roles, approving administrator identity, resulting Google account identity, unfinished-operation progress, notification outcomes, and outstanding-assignment revisions only as needed for the workflow. Use Drizzle migrations and database-backed uniqueness/concurrency controls. Do not store parallel authoritative Workspace profiles or application-owned administrator roles.\\n- **Concurrent actions and uncertain results:** Repeated submissions, double-clicks, simultaneous review, retries, process interruption, and external API timeouts must not create conflicting decisions or duplicate accounts. Persist and reconcile durable provisioning progress with Google before treating an uncertain create outcome as safe to repeat. An address match by itself is insufficient to claim ownership of an account.\\n- **Partial provisioning recovery:** Create the account and establish selected initial memberships. Keep successful work on failure, show the unfinished operations, and let an authorized administrator retry only unfinished steps. Never automatically delete an account to roll back failed onboarding. Recovery uses the current acting administrator's permitted credentials, not a stored stronger identity.\\n- **Assignment revisions:** An authorized administrator may explicitly remove or replace an unfinished assignment when a group disappears or becomes unmanageable. Preserve already-successful memberships, record the revision while the workflow is active, and require confirmation. Do not silently drop failed groups. All currently selected memberships must succeed before handover; zero remaining selections is valid when explicitly confirmed.\\n- **Account handover:** Show Awaiting handover only after provisioning succeeds. The administrator performs Google's Admin console existing-user password-reset/email flow with a required password change, then confirms in the website that instructions were sent. Do not email plaintext passwords through the application. Confirmation is not proof of inbox delivery or first sign-in.\\n- **Notifications:** Notify the configured Google Group when a verified request enters the review queue using minimal personal information and a review link. Its membership controls delivery only. Notification failure does not prevent queue entry, erase the request, or undo account creation; show failure and permit retry. Retain rejection form data until its outcome notification succeeds.\\n- **Cleanup and retained records:** Delete unverified submissions at their deadline, expire unreviewed verified requests at their deadline, delete rejected applicant profiles after successful outcome notification, and delete successful applicant form data after provisioning and confirmed handover. Retain only the minimal operational record identifying the resulting Google account and approving administrator after success. Active recovery/revision records must not become a permanent second profile system. Cleanup must be resumable and must not delete active accepted-request recovery state.\\n- **Deployment configuration:** Configure the Workspace organization/domain, Google OAuth grants/redirects, sender mailbox, notification group, Neon connection, and Turnstile site key, secret, and permitted hostnames. Keep secrets out of Git. Missing required production configuration cannot silently enable mock operations or bypass validation.\\n- **Repository delivery:** Implement through the repository's worktree and stacked-PR workflow: frontend development first, complete application/database/CAPTCHA functionality next, and Google Workspace integration last. Use small functional commits, review for correctness and unnecessary complexity, run meaningful checks/lint/formatting, and link resulting PRs back to this issue.\\n\\n## Testing Decisions\\n\\n- **User-confirmed seam:** Test the running website at its public interface. Browser flows are the primary seam; use the same application HTTP boundary for concurrency, repeated actions, and cleanup checks. Avoid separate suites for every internal helper or component.\\n- **What makes a good test:** Assert externally observable behavior and meaningful consequences: available actions, validation messages, lifecycle visibility, delivered email contents, account/membership operations, retained successful work, retry outcomes, and cleanup boundaries. Do not assert component hierarchy, private helper calls, internal state-machine representation, or incidental request ordering.\\n- **Existing prior art:** The starter application has no existing automated tests, test runner configuration, onboarding implementation, or established test seam to reuse. Introduce the smallest browser/HTTP acceptance setup needed for these scenarios, rather than a broad testing framework or redundant per-layer suites.\\n- **Frontend-first development checks:** Temporary deterministic HTTP fixtures may be used while composing screens. Cover signup validation and CAPTCHA feedback; verification/resend outcomes; loading/empty/error queue states; corrected information; Workspace address collision; zero/multiple groups and explicit roles; review confirmation; rejection; provisioning failure/retry; unfinished-assignment revision; notification failure/retry; and manual handover confirmation. Include keyboard operation, labels, focus/error feedback, and responsive layout checks. Fixture-only checks do not establish first-stage completion.\\n- **Initial-stage application checks:** Run those browser scenarios against real application endpoints and an isolated real Postgres database before Google Workspace integration. Simulate Google-dependent OAuth/Directory/Gmail responses at their external boundary; use captured messages to exercise real email-verification links. Do not replace application session/authorization handling, workflow orchestration, persistence, or server-side CAPTCHA validation with mocks. Exercise Turnstile validation through its external boundary using its documented testing configuration or deterministic service responses, and verify real configured Turnstile integration before claiming the initial-stage CAPTCHA deliverable is ready. Recorded Google simulations establish expected application behavior, not real Workspace provisioning or email delivery.\\n- **Later Google integration checks:** Reuse the same browser/HTTP scenarios after replacing the Google simulations with real integrations. The already-implemented database, CAPTCHA, concurrency controls, verification, review, notification retry, and retention behavior must continue to pass. Keep deterministic provider-failure scenarios at the external service boundary; validate actual Google permissions and transport with the live-tenant smoke check.\\n- **Protected actions:** Verify that missing/invalid/expired/replayed CAPTCHA or Siteverify failure cannot create a submission or send a resend email, while verification-link clicks remain CAPTCHA-free. Verify rejected credentials, unavailable own-profile lookup, unauthorized management, and Google-denied operations cannot proceed or fall back to stronger credentials.\\n- **Verification and retention:** Check verification deadlines, non-extending resends, duplicate verification races, reapplication, verified review eligibility, the 30-day review deadline, rejection-notification-before-deletion, completion-before-deletion, and retention of accepted partial-provisioning/handover state. Arrange aged persisted records rather than waiting for wall-clock deadlines.\\n- **Concurrency and recovery:** Issue parallel or repeated operations through the application HTTP boundary. Demonstrate at most one active verified request, a single accepted outcome/account, address-collision rejection without existing-account attachment, recovery after an uncertain create response, partial group failure retaining successful assignments, explicit revision of unfinished groups, and no automatic account deletion.\\n- **Handover and notifications:** Prove handover remains blocked until selected memberships succeed, completion requires explicit confirmation, and email failures neither lose requests nor undo provisioned accounts. Confirm notification-group membership and group manager/owner roles never grant administrative authority.\\n- **Live Workspace smoke check:** With configured tenant credentials, verify real OAuth permissions, administrator resource restrictions, group roles, sender/notification-group delivery, the Admin console handover flow, and suspended/deleted-account behavior with existing sessions. This check is separate from deterministic automated acceptance tests because mocked services cannot establish actual tenant policy or Google propagation timing.\\n- **Phase gates:** Initial-stage completion requires the frontend running against real application endpoints with working database persistence, CAPTCHA, verification workflow, review transitions, concurrency/recovery state, notification handling, and retention/cleanup, plus appropriate browser/HTTP security/concurrency/retention checks and build/lint/format checks. Only Google-dependent external responses may remain simulated. Later integration completion requires the reused checks and a documented live-tenant Google smoke-check outcome. Do not label a UI-only mock, fake database, or widget-only CAPTCHA implementation as first-stage completion, or simulated Google operations as production integration.\\n\\n## Out of Scope\\n\\n- Applicant accounts, applicant sign-in, or an applicant dashboard.\\n- Ongoing Workspace user management, suspension, deletion, existing-user group management, or group creation/deletion.\\n- Self-service contact changes and profile change requests, although existing ADRs still govern future work.\\n- Multiple Workspace organizations, localization, or a language switcher.\\n- Automatic initial group assignments or onboarding into pre-existing accounts based solely on email matches.\\n- Application-owned administrator roles, parallel authoritative Workspace profiles, domain-wide delegation, or stronger-credential fallback.\\n- Automatic plaintext-password emails, automatic handover confirmation, proof of inbox delivery, or first-login tracking.\\n- Reversal of successful provisioning through automatic account deletion.\\n- Hosting tier management, automatic provider upgrades, independent backups beyond the existing hosting decision, or speculative generalized infrastructure.\\n\\n## Further Notes\\n\\n- This specification synthesizes the completed design conversation. The user confirmed the website-boundary testing seam and clarified that frontend-first development must include the non-Google backend functionality before the later Google Workspace integration; further product interviewing is not required.\\n- Respect the domain glossary and the existing decisions: Google-authoritative identity/profiles, acting-administrator OAuth with the dedicated sender grant, and Vercel/Neon hosting. The release includes onboarding only; broader documented capabilities remain future scope.\\n- The existing issue is being updated rather than creating a competing specification. It is ready for implementation once labeled ready-for-agent.\\n- The initial stage needs real database and Turnstile configuration and must be independently runnable without Google Workspace credentials, with clearly development-only Google simulations. Full deployment additionally needs organization-specific Google configuration and OAuth grants. The dedicated Gmail transport is part of the deferred Google integration; email preparation, verification tokens, captured-message testing, notification state, and retry behavior are implemented in the initial stage rather than switching the agreed production email provider.\\n- Cloudflare's server-side validation documentation establishes the Siteverify requirement, five-minute token lifetime, and single-use behavior: https://developers.cloudflare.com/turnstile/get-started/server-side-validation/.\\n- A manual account-handover step is deliberate. Google remains authoritative, and the website records an administrator's confirmation that instructions were sent.\\n\",\"number\":1,\"title\":\"Implement Google Workspace signup review and onboarding\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/itsmeyaw/binden/issues/1\"}\n{\"body\":\"## Parent\\n\\n#1\\n\\n## What to build\\n\\nDeliver a public applicant path that submits one signup request through the running website. It persists the request in Neon Postgres through Drizzle, validates fields on the server, validates Cloudflare Turnstile server-side, prevents a competing active request, and records a verification message in local mail capture.\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n\\n- [ ] An applicant can submit required and optional data from mobile or desktop with accessible field-level validation and progress feedback.\\n- [ ] Invalid, expired, replayed, or unverifiable Turnstile tokens cannot create a signup request, and recoverable failures retain entered values.\\n- [ ] The persisted application prevents a duplicate submission from overwriting an active signup request and exposes a clear outcome.\\n- [ ] A captured verification message is created without requiring Google Workspace credentials.\\n\\n## Blocked by\\n\\nNone - can start immediately\",\"comments\":[{\"id\":\"IC_kwDOU7HV788AAAABZGOn9A\",\"author\":{\"login\":\"itsmeyaw\"},\"authorAssociation\":\"OWNER\",\"body\":\"Implemented the protected public signup path in stack #16. Persistence and migration: #13. Zod validation, server-side Turnstile verification, atomic mail-capture write, and HTTP tests: #14. Landing sign-in/signup choice and the sign-up form: #15. Checks passed: formatting, TypeScript, focused signup tests, Oxlint, production build, and browser route/mobile smoke checks. Real Neon writes require a configured DATABASE_URL.\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-10-04T10:55:04Z\",\"includesCreatedEdit\":true,\"isMinimized\":false,\"minimizedReason\":\"\",\"reactionGroups\":[],\"url\":\"https://github.com/itsmeyaw/binden/issues/2#issuecomment-5979219956\",\"viewerDidAuthor\":true},{\"id\":\"IC_kwDOU7HV788AAAABZGT9cg\",\"author\":{\"login\":\"itsmeyaw\"},\"authorAssociation\":\"OWNER\",\"body\":\"Follow-up: PR #15 now explicitly approves the required esbuild install scripts in the pnpm 11 workspace policy, so pnpm install and pnpm dev run normally.\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-10-04T11:07:27Z\",\"includesCreatedEdit\":false,\"isMinimized\":false,\"minimizedReason\":\"\",\"reactionGroups\":[],\"url\":\"https://github.com/itsmeyaw/binden/issues/2#issuecomment-5979307378\",\"viewerDidAuthor\":true},{\"id\":\"IC_kwDOU7HV788AAAABZGWswQ\",\"author\":{\"login\":\"itsmeyaw\"},\"authorAssociation\":\"OWNER\",\"body\":\"Follow-up: PR #15 now loads .env.local and .env for drizzle-kit, so the local DATABASE_URL is available when running pnpm exec drizzle-kit migrate.\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-10-04T11:13:55Z\",\"includesCreatedEdit\":false,\"isMinimized\":false,\"minimizedReason\":\"\",\"reactionGroups\":[],\"url\":\"https://github.com/itsmeyaw/binden/issues/2#issuecomment-5979352257\",\"viewerDidAuthor\":true},{\"id\":\"IC_kwDOU7HV788AAAABZG--fQ\",\"author\":{\"login\":\"itsmeyaw\"},\"authorAssociation\":\"OWNER\",\"body\":\"Follow-up: PR #15 now forces the Turnstile widget to the light theme and adds extra bottom padding below the submit action.\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-10-04T12:39:31Z\",\"includesCreatedEdit\":false,\"isMinimized\":false,\"minimizedReason\":\"\",\"reactionGroups\":[],\"url\":\"https://github.com/itsmeyaw/binden/issues/2#issuecomment-5980012157\",\"viewerDidAuthor\":true},{\"id\":\"IC_kwDOU7HV788AAAABZHB9Ew\",\"author\":{\"login\":\"itsmeyaw\"},\"authorAssociation\":\"OWNER\",\"body\":\"Follow-up: PR #15 now follows shadcn `signup-01` layout: a full-viewport centered `max-w-sm` form card with a full-width in-form submit action.\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-10-04T12:45:01Z\",\"includesCreatedEdit\":false,\"isMinimized\":false,\"minimizedReason\":\"\",\"reactionGroups\":[],\"url\":\"https://github.com/itsmeyaw/binden/issues/2#issuecomment-5980060947\",\"viewerDidAuthor\":true},{\"id\":\"IC_kwDOU7HV788AAAABZHMXLA\",\"author\":{\"login\":\"itsmeyaw\"},\"authorAssociation\":\"OWNER\",\"body\":\"Follow-up: PR #15 now uses the shadcn `login-05` layout on the front page, branded for PM3 Muenchen e.V. with a `Login with Google Workspace` action and account-request link.\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-10-04T13:03:26Z\",\"includesCreatedEdit\":false,\"isMinimized\":false,\"minimizedReason\":\"\",\"reactionGroups\":[],\"url\":\"https://github.com/itsmeyaw/binden/issues/2#issuecomment-5980231468\",\"viewerDidAuthor\":true}],\"number\":2,\"title\":\"Submit a protected signup request\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/itsmeyaw/binden/issues/2\"}\n",
