            "output": "<path>/Users/itsmeyaw/projects/binden/node_modules/next/dist/docs/01-app/03-api-reference/03-file-conventions/page.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ---\n2: title: page.js\n3: description: API reference for the page.js file.\n4: ---\n5: \n6: The `page` file allows you to define UI that is **unique** to a route. You can create a page by default exporting a component from the file:\n7: \n8: ```tsx filename=\"app/blog/[slug]/page.tsx\" switcher\n9: export default function Page({\n10:   params,\n11:   searchParams,\n12: }: {\n13:   params: Promise<{ slug: string }>\n14:   searchParams: Promise<{ [key: string]: string | string[] | undefined }>\n15: }) {\n16:   return <h1>My Page</h1>\n17: }\n18: ```\n19: \n20: ```jsx filename=\"app/blog/[slug]/page.js\" switcher\n21: export default function Page({ params, searchParams }) {\n22:   return <h1>My Page</h1>\n23: }\n24: ```\n25: \n26: ## Good to know\n27: \n28: - The `.js`, `.jsx`, or `.tsx` file extensions can be used for `page`.\n29: - A `page` is always the **leaf** of the route subtree.\n30: - A `page` file is required to make a route segment **publicly accessible**.\n31: - Pages are [Server Components](https://react.dev/reference/rsc/server-components) by default, but can be set to a [Client Component](https://react.dev/reference/rsc/use-client).\n32: - In the [component hierarchy](/docs/app/getting-started/project-structure#component-hierarchy), `page.js` is the innermost file convention. It is wrapped by `loading.js` (Suspense boundary), `error.js` (error boundary), `template.js`, and `layout.js` in the same segment.\n33: \n34: ## Reference\n35: \n36: ### Props\n37: \n38: #### `params` (optional)\n39: \n40: A promise that resolves to an object containing the [dynamic route parameters](/docs/app/api-reference/file-conventions/dynamic-routes) from the root segment down to that page.\n41: \n42: ```tsx filename=\"app/shop/[slug]/page.tsx\" switcher\n43: export default async function Page({\n44:   params,\n45: }: {\n46:   params: Promise<{ slug: string }>\n47: }) {\n48:   const { slug } = await params\n49: }\n50: ```\n51: \n52: ```jsx filename=\"app/shop/[slug]/page.js\" switcher\n53: export default async function Page({ params }) {\n54:   const { slug } = await params\n55: }\n56: ```\n57: \n58: | Example Route                        | URL         | `params`                                |\n59: | ------------------------------------ | ----------- | --------------------------------------- |\n60: | `app/shop/[slug]/page.js`            | `/shop/1`   | `Promise<{ slug: '1' }>`                |\n61: | `app/shop/[category]/[item]/page.js` | `/shop/1/2` | `Promise<{ category: '1', item: '2' }>` |\n62: | `app/shop/[...slug]/page.js`         | `/shop/1/2` | `Promise<{ slug: ['1', '2'] }>`         |\n63: \n64: - Since the `params` prop is a promise, you must use `async/await` or React's [`use`](https://react.dev/reference/react/use) function to access the values.\n65:   - In version 14 and earlier, `params` was a synchronous prop. To help with backwards compatibility, you can still access it synchronously in Next.js 15, but this behavior will be deprecated in the future.\n66: \n67: #### `searchParams` (optional)\n68: \n69: A promise that resolves to an object containing the [search parameters](https://developer.mozilla.org/docs/Learn/Common_questions/What_is_a_URL#parameters) of the current URL. For example:\n70: \n71: ```tsx filename=\"app/shop/page.tsx\" switcher\n72: export default async function Page({\n73:   searchParams,\n74: }: {\n75:   searchParams: Promise<{ [key: string]: string | string[] | undefined }>\n76: }) {\n77:   const filters = (await searchParams).filters\n78: }\n79: ```\n80: \n81: ```jsx filename=\"app/shop/page.js\" switcher\n82: export default async function Page({ searchParams }) {\n83:   const filters = (await searchParams).filters\n84: }\n85: ```\n86: \n87: Client Component **pages** can also access `searchParams` using React’s [`use`](https://react.dev/reference/react/use) hook:\n88: \n89: ```tsx filename=\"app/shop/page.tsx\" switcher\n90: 'use client'\n91: import { use } from 'react'\n92: \n93: export default function Page({\n94:   searchParams,\n95: }: {\n96:   searchParams: Promise<{ [key: string]: string | string[] | undefined }>\n97: }) {\n98:   const filters = use(searchParams).filters\n99: }\n100: ```\n101: \n102: ```jsx filename=\"app/page.jsx\" switcher\n103: 'use client'\n104: import { use } from 'react'\n105: \n106: export default function Page({ searchParams }) {\n107:   const filters = use(searchParams).filters\n108: }\n109: ```\n110: \n111: | Example URL     | `searchParams`                |\n112: | --------------- | ----------------------------- |\n113: | `/shop?a=1`     | `Promise<{ a: '1' }>`         |\n114: | `/shop?a=1&b=2` | `Promise<{ a: '1', b: '2' }>` |\n115: | `/shop?a=1&a=2` | `Promise<{ a: ['1', '2'] }>`  |\n116: \n117: - Since the `searchParams` prop is a promise. You must use `async/await` or React's [`use`](https://react.dev/reference/react/use) function to access the values.\n118:   - In version 14 and earlier, `searchParams` was a synchronous prop. To help with backwards compatibility, you can still access it synchronously in Next.js 15, but this behavior will be deprecated in the future.\n119: - `searchParams` is a **[Request-time API](/docs/app/glossary#request-time-apis)** whose values cannot be known ahead of time. Using it will opt the page into **[dynamic rendering](/docs/app/glossary#dynamic-rendering)** at request time.\n120: - With [Cache Components](/docs/app/getting-started/caching), where you access `searchParams` in the component tree determines how much of the page can be prerendered. See [Maximizing the static shell](/docs/app/getting-started/caching#maximizing-the-static-shell).\n121: - `searchParams` is a plain JavaScript object, not a `URLSearchParams` instance.\n122: \n123: ### Page Props Helper\n124: \n125: You can type pages with `PageProps` to get strongly typed `params` and `searchParams` from the route literal. `PageProps` is a globally available helper.\n126: \n127: ```tsx filename=\"app/blog/[slug]/page.tsx\"\n128: export default async function Page(props: PageProps<'/blog/[slug]'>) {\n129:   const { slug } = await props.params\n130:   const query = await props.searchParams\n131:   return <h1>Blog Post: {slug}</h1>\n132: }\n133: ```\n134: \n135: > **Good to know**\n136: >\n137: > - Using a literal route (e.g. `'/blog/[slug]'`) enables autocomplete and strict keys for `params`.\n138: > - Static routes resolve `params` to `{}`.\n139: > - Types are generated during `next dev`, `next build`, or with `next typegen`.\n140: > - After type generation, the `PageProps` helper is globally available. It doesn't need to be imported.\n141: \n142: ## Examples\n143: \n144: ### Displaying content based on `params`\n145: \n146: Using [dynamic route segments](/docs/app/api-reference/file-conventions/dynamic-routes), you can display or fetch specific content for the page based on the `params` prop.\n147: \n148: ```tsx filename=\"app/blog/[slug]/page.tsx\" switcher\n149: export default async function Page({\n150:   params,\n151: }: {\n152:   params: Promise<{ slug: string }>\n153: }) {\n154:   const { slug } = await params\n155:   return <h1>Blog Post: {slug}</h1>\n156: }\n157: ```\n158: \n159: ```jsx filename=\"app/blog/[slug]/page.js\" switcher\n160: export default async function Page({ params }) {\n161:   const { slug } = await params\n162:   return <h1>Blog Post: {slug}</h1>\n163: }\n164: ```\n165: \n166: ### Handling filtering with `searchParams`\n167: \n168: You can use the `searchParams` prop to handle filtering, pagination, or sorting based on the query string of the URL.\n169: \n170: ```tsx filename=\"app/shop/page.tsx\" switcher\n171: export default async function Page({\n172:   searchParams,\n173: }: {\n174:   searchParams: Promise<{ [key: string]: string | string[] | undefined }>\n175: }) {\n176:   const { page = '1', sort = 'asc', query = '' } = await searchParams\n177: \n178:   return (\n179:     <div>\n180:       <h1>Product Listing</h1>\n181:       <p>Search query: {query}</p>\n182:       <p>Current page: {page}</p>\n183:       <p>Sort order: {sort}</p>\n184:     </div>\n185:   )\n186: }\n187: ```\n188: \n189: ```jsx filename=\"app/shop/page.js\" switcher\n190: export default async function Page({ searchParams }) {\n191:   const { page = '1', sort = 'asc', query = '' } = await searchParams\n192: \n193:   return (\n194:     <div>\n195:       <h1>Product Listing</h1>\n196:       <p>Search query: {query}</p>\n197:       <p>Current page: {page}</p>\n198:       <p>Sort order: {sort}</p>\n199:     </div>\n200:   )\n201: }\n202: ```\n203: \n204: ### Reading `searchParams` and `params` in Client Components\n205: \n206: To use `searchParams` and `params` in a Client Component (which cannot be `async`), you can use React's [`use`](https://react.dev/reference/react/use) function to read the promise:\n207: \n208: ```tsx filename=\"app/page.tsx\" switcher\n209: 'use client'\n210: \n211: import { use } from 'react'\n212: \n213: export default function Page({\n214:   params,\n215:   searchParams,\n216: }: {\n217:   params: Promise<{ slug: string }>\n218:   searchParams: Promise<{ [key: string]: string | string[] | undefined }>\n219: }) {\n220:   const { slug } = use(params)\n221:   const { query } = use(searchParams)\n222: }\n223: ```\n224: \n225: ```js filename=\"app/page.js\" switcher\n226: 'use client'\n227: \n228: import { use } from 'react'\n229: \n230: export default function Page({ params, searchParams }) {\n231:   const { slug } = use(params)\n232:   const { query } = use(searchParams)\n233: }\n234: ```\n235: \n236: ## Version History\n237: \n238: | Version      | Changes                                                                                                          |\n239: | ------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n240: | `v15.0.0-RC` | `params` and `searchParams` are now promises. A [codemod](/docs/app/guides/upgrading/codemods#150) is available. |\n241: | `v13.0.0`    | `page` introduced.                                                                                               |\n\n(End of file - total 241 lines)\n</content>",
