            "output": "<path>/Users/itsmeyaw/projects/binden/.agents/skills/gh-stack/references/stack-design.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Designing a stack\n2: \n3: How to decide what goes in each layer. Read this before running `gh stack init`.\n4: \n5: ## Contents\n6: \n7: - [Plan the layers before writing code](#plan-the-layers-before-writing-code)\n8: - [Branch naming](#branch-naming)\n9: - [Staging changes deliberately](#staging-changes-deliberately)\n10: - [When to add a layer](#when-to-add-a-layer)\n11: - [One stack, one story](#one-stack-one-story)\n12: \n13: ## Plan the layers before writing code\n14: \n15: A stack is a dependency chain. If code in one layer depends on code in another, the dependency must\n16: live in the same branch or a lower one. That constraint is much cheaper to satisfy by planning than\n17: by restructuring later, because there is no non-interactive in-place reorder — fixing the order\n18: means`unstack` and `init` again.\n19: \n20: Decide the layers first, then write code into them:\n21: \n22: ```\n23: (main) <- todo-app/models <- todo-app/api <- todo-app/frontend <- todo-app/integration\n24: ```\n25: \n26: - `todo-app/models` — shared types and schema\n27: - `todo-app/api` — routes that use the models\n28: - `todo-app/frontend` — components that call the routes\n29: - `todo-app/integration` — tests exercising the whole feature\n30: \n31: This is illustrative. Infer the stack topic and layer names from the actual task; do not reuse\n32: `todo-app` or these layer names literally.\n33: \n34: The failure mode to avoid is writing everything on one branch and trying to split it afterwards.\n35: If a task is large enough to warrant a stack, create the stack at the start.\n36: \n37: ## Branch naming\n38: \n39: Prefer a shared topic prefix plus the layer's concern:\n40: `<topic>/<concern>` — for example, `billing/schema`, `billing/api`, `billing/ui`.\n41: This keeps related branches recognizable without using generic names that could belong to any\n42: stack. **User and repository branch naming conventions take precedence; follow them instead.**\n43: \n44: Names are used exactly as given — nothing is prepended or transformed, and slashes are kept, so\n45: `gh stack add refactor/foo` creates a branch literally named `refactor/foo`.\n46: \n47: If you pass `-m` without a branch name, the name is generated from the commit message in\n48: date-and-slug form (for example `03-24-add_api_routes`). Prefer naming the branch yourself.\n49: \n50: ## Staging changes deliberately\n51: \n52: Use `git add` and `git commit` directly rather than the `add -Am` shortcut. The point is control\n53: over which changes land in which branch. With several modified files in the working tree, stage the\n54: subset that belongs to the current layer, commit it, then create the next branch and stage the rest\n55: there:\n56: \n57: ```bash\n58: git add internal/models/user.go internal/models/session.go\n59: git commit -m \"Add user and session models\"\n60: \n61: gh stack add api-routes\n62: git add internal/api/routes.go internal/api/handlers.go\n63: git commit -m \"Add user API routes\"\n64: ```\n65: \n66: Multiple commits per branch are fine. What matters is that every commit in a branch serves the same\n67: concern, and that a change belonging to a different concern goes in a different branch.\n68: \n69: Note that `gh stack add <branch>` without `-Am` does not touch the working tree, so uncommitted\n70: changes carry over to the new branch. Commit or stash first if you want the new layer to start clean.\n71: \n72: ## When to add a layer\n73: \n74: Add a branch when you start a **different concern that depends on what you have built so far**.\n75: Signals:\n76: \n77: - Moving from backend to frontend, or from core logic to tests or documentation\n78: - The next changes have a different reviewer audience\n79: - The current branch's diff is already large enough to review on its own\n80: \n81: A layer that cannot be described in one sentence is usually two layers.\n82: \n83: ## One stack, one story\n84: \n85: A stack should read as a coherent progression: a reviewer walks the PRs bottom to top and sees the\n86: feature being built.\n87: \n88: **Use a single stack** when every branch serves the same feature or project, even if the layers span\n89: different concerns.\n90: \n91: **Start a separate stack** for unrelated work — a different feature, an unrelated bug fix, an\n92: independent refactor. Do not mix efforts into one stack just because you happened to work on both.\n93: Use `gh stack init` for the new effort, or `gh stack checkout <target>` to move between existing\n94: stacks.\n95: \n96: A trivial incidental fix can ride along in the current stack. Once it grows into its own project, it\n97: deserves its own stack.\n\n(End of file - total 97 lines)\n</content>",
